# Overview

dayforeit — project guardrails v1
(applies to all modules, features, and future work)

1. product constitution (locked)

day fore it’s product behaviour is defined by canonical documentation and scenario-driven design.
these rules apply to all features, not just trips.

1.1 canonical sources of truth
before proposing, modifying, or refactoring any product behaviour (including trip creation, trip management, rsvp, logistics, exports, scoring, or related ui/logic), you must read and respect:

- docs/v1.md (single source of truth for product constitution)
- docs/trips/scenarios.md (scenario definitions)
- docs/trips/iteration-playbook.md (iteration guidelines)
- docs/trips/ai-scenario-assist.md (ai assistance rules)

docs/v1.md is the authoritative product truth. trip-specific scenario docs supplement v1.md.

1.2 non-negotiable product principles
- minimum prompts beats completeness
- phase ≠ form
  - phase is time-based
  - scenarios decide modules and steps
- no dead ends
  every screen must have:
  - a next action
  - a skip
  - an exit
- defaults first; ask later; never ask twice
- do not re-invent flows that already exist
- do not introduce new prompts, steps, or forms without:
  - mapping them to an existing scenario, or
  - explicitly adding a new scenario via the iteration playbook

1.3 scenario enforcement
any change to trip coordination must explicitly state:
- which scenariokey(s) it affects
- whether it changes:
  - scenario classification
  - coordination sequence
  - readiness rules
- why it does not introduce:
  - loops
  - autoprompter behaviour

1.4 cross-border agent scenario (locked)
for the cross_border_agent (batam) scenario, the following rules are immutable:

- trip creation requires only:
  - date
  - course
- agent export must include:
  - passport_full_name
  - passport_number
  - passport_nationality
  - passport_date_of_birth
  - passport_expiry_date
  - handicap
- flights can only be generated after sign-ups close
- quartile method is the default
- manual flight edits must persist

1.5 ai assistance constraints
ai may assist only as defined in docs/trips/ai-scenario-assist.md (see also docs/v1.md section 6 for core flows):

- ai may propose:
  - scenarioanswers
  - confidence only
- deterministic classifier decides scenario_key
- ai must never:
  - write to the database
  - skip required steps

2. engineering guardrails

these rules govern how work is executed safely across the entire project.

2.1 data and persistence
- no local storage
- always use the database
- database schema is defined in docs/schema.md and is authoritative (see also docs/v1.md section 8 for data model summary)
- do not invent fields or types not listed in the schema

2.2 supabase setup (do not change unless asked)
- uses supabase auth + postgres
- auth methods:
  - google oauth
  - magic link
- uses @supabase/ssr with cookie-based auth
- environment variables (names only):
  - next_public_supabase_url
  - next_public_supabase_anon_key
- service role key exists but must never be used client-side
- createsupabaseserverclient() is async and must be awaited
- cookies may only be written in:
  - route handlers
  - server actions
- rls is currently off (do not enable unless explicitly asked)

2.3 code safety and discipline
- prefer minimal diffs over full rewrites
- never rewrite an entire file unless explicitly asked
- do not refactor or rename files/functions for cleanliness unless requested
- follow existing architecture patterns and file-local style
- when unsure about a type or field:
  - search the codebase for the source of truth
  - do not guess
- always keep builds green:
  - ensure typescript correctness
  - avoid introducing mismatched types
- make changes in:
  - small
  - reversible
  - well-scoped commits
- suggest commit messages

3. collaboration protocol (you ↔ cursor)

these rules govern how work is proposed and executed.

3.1 planning before execution
- always explain what you will do before making changes
- always ask whether you should proceed
- if editing more than ~30 lines:
  - summarise proposed changes first

3.2 language and presentation
- all user-facing ui text must use sentence case
- always use uk english

3.3 conflict handling
if a requested change conflicts with any rule in this document, you must:
- flag the conflict
- explain why
- stop before proceeding

